window.UESCA_CONTENT['English/AppA-RunET.pdf'] = `<div class="content-body">
<h1>Appendix A: Running Lingo & Etiquette</h1>
<blockquote style="border-left:4px solid #00a86b;padding:12px 16px;margin:16px 0;background:#f0faf5;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><em><strong>MATERIAL IN THIS SECTION WILL NOT BE TESTED ON</strong></em></blockquote>
<hr style="border:none;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;margin:28px 0">
<h2 class="section-head">Running Lingo</h2>
<p>As with most sports or industries, the sport of running has its own lingo that as a coach you need to be familiar with.</p>
<p><strong>5K:</strong> Common distance of a running race (3.1 miles)</p>
<p><strong>10K:</strong> Common distance of running race (6.2 miles)</p>
<p><strong>13.1:</strong> Distance in miles of a half-marathon</p>
<p><strong>26.2:</strong> Distance in miles of a marathon</p>
<p><strong>AG:</strong> Age Group</p>
<p><strong>Aid Station:</strong> Locations that are spaced throughout a race where participants can receive fuel, hydration and first aid</p>
<p><strong>AT:</strong> Anaerobic Threshold</p>
<p><strong>Bonk:</strong> Also called "hitting the wall". Occurs when the glycogen stores in the body are empty and the athlete suffers extreme fatigue and the body's energy source essentially shuts down.</p>
<p><strong>Chip:</strong> An electronic chip that tracks the athlete throughout the race</p>
<p><strong>Chute:</strong> Sometimes called the "finishing chute". This is the area directly after the end of the race that athletes funnel into</p>
<p><strong>Corral:</strong> Designated areas where runners line up prior to the start of a race. Usually denoted by a runner's estimated pace</p>
<p><strong>Draft:</strong> To run behind another person to save energy</p>
<p><strong>Dropped:</strong> If a runner cannot keep up with a group of runners and has been left behind, they have been "dropped"</p>
<p><strong>DNF:</strong> Did Not Finish</p>
<p><strong>DNS:</strong> Did Not Start</p>
<p><strong>DQ:</strong> Disqualified</p>
<p><strong>Fartlek:</strong> Type of running workout that integrates random speed changes</p>
<p><strong>Gait:</strong> Denotes the form of a runner and in particular, the legs</p>
<p><strong>Half:</strong> Half distance marathon (13.1 miles)</p>
<p><strong>HR:</strong> Heart rate</p>
<p><strong>HRM:</strong> Heart rate monitor</p>
<p><strong>Kick:</strong> Used to describe the sprint to the finish line</p>
<p><strong>Leg Turnover:</strong> Used to denote the running stride speed (i.e. fast, slow)</p>
<p><strong>LT:</strong> Lactate Threshold</p>
<p><strong>Marshall:</strong> Individuals on the course to ensure the safety of the participants as well as to ensure all participants follow the rules</p>
<p><strong>Pace:</strong> Denotes min/mile rate for a set duration of time or distance</p>
<p><strong>Pack:</strong> Group of runners</p>
<p><strong>Pick Ups:</strong> Short bursts of speed or accelerations during a run</p>
<p><strong>Plyos:</strong> Short for "Plyometrics", meaning explosive, jumping style movements. Meant to build power</p>
<p><strong>PR:</strong> Personal Record (i.e. best time at a specific distance event)</p>
<p><strong>Repeats:</strong> A type of interval. Repeated up tempo efforts (typically of the same distance) with a rest period in between</p>
<p><strong>Strides:</strong> Short sprints often done prior to, or after a run</p>
<p><strong>Tempo:</strong> A steady-state effort often associated with running at a pace close to one's LT.</p>
<p><strong>VO2:</strong> Commonly refers to the "VO2 Max Test or one's aerobic capacity based on the test</p>
<p><strong>VT:</strong> Ventilatory Threshold</p>
<p><strong>Wave:</strong> Large running events often combine runners of similar paces in a group, called a 'wave.' Waves are often broken down further into 'corrals.'</p>
<hr style="border:none;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;margin:28px 0">
<h2 class="section-head">Running Etiquette</h2>
<ul>
<li>During track workouts, if recovering or running slowly, stay to outside lanes</li>
<li>If getting water or food at aid stations during a race, quickly select items but do not stop in this area. Keep it moving.</li>
<li>If not getting food or water at aid stations, run on the far left to make room for those that are getting food or water</li>
<li>At the finish line, do not stop at the line. Keep moving through to not clog up the finish area</li>
<li>If walking during a race, stay to the far right of the road</li>
</ul>
<h3>General</h3>
<ul>
<li>Don't litter</li>
<li>Do not urinate in public. Use porta-potty's and, or bathrooms allocated by the event for use</li>
<li>If waiting in line for a porta-potty, allow participants with earlier start times than you ahead of you</li>
<li>Be on time for group training sessions</li>
<li>Respect and follow the workout type for group training sessions</li>
<li>No swearing at event</li>
<li>Thank the race director, sponsors and volunteers at events</li>
<li>Cheer on other participants</li>
</ul>
<hr style="border:none;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;margin:28px 0">
<h2 class="section-head">Figures</h2>
<figure style="text-align:center;margin:24px 0"><img src="images/AppA-RunET/p001_i01.png" alt="Page 1 figure" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.12)"><figcaption style="font-size:13px;color:#64748b;margin-top:8px"><em>Page 1 figure</em></figcaption></figure>
<figure style="text-align:center;margin:24px 0"><img src="images/AppA-RunET/p001_i02.png" alt="Page 1 figure" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.12)"><figcaption style="font-size:13px;color:#64748b;margin-top:8px"><em>Page 1 figure</em></figcaption></figure>
<figure style="text-align:center;margin:24px 0"><img src="images/AppA-RunET/p001_i03.png" alt="Page 1 figure" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.12)"><figcaption style="font-size:13px;color:#64748b;margin-top:8px"><em>Page 1 figure</em></figcaption></figure>
</div>`;

